1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,684
Look at your little fingernail.

2
00:00:02,684 --> 00:00:08,519
Right now, about sixty-five billion tiny
particles from the Sun are passing straight
through it.

3
00:00:08,519 --> 00:00:11,802
Every second. Many flew through the whole
Earth first.

4
00:00:11,802 --> 00:00:13,078
And you feel nothing.

5
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:17,614
They are called neutrinos, the ghost
particles.

6
00:00:17,614 --> 00:00:21,109
So how do you catch something that passes
through a planet?

7
00:00:21,109 --> 00:00:24,485
This year, the Nobel Prize in Physics went
to one answer:

8
00:00:24,485 --> 00:00:26,796
a giant block of ice at the South Pole.

9
00:00:33,279 --> 00:00:35,012
The story starts in 1930.

10
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:39,309
Wolfgang Pauli saw energy going missing when
some atoms decay.

11
00:00:39,309 --> 00:00:45,409
As a desperate remedy, he proposed a tiny
neutral particle that carries it away,
unseen.

12
00:00:45,409 --> 00:00:48,459
It took twenty-six years to prove him right.

13
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:54,629
In 1956, Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines
caught neutrinos coming from a nuclear
reactor.

14
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:57,662
Why so hard?

15
00:00:57,662 --> 00:01:04,364
A neutrino has no electric charge and almost
no mass, so it almost never touches the
matter it flies through.

16
00:01:04,364 --> 00:01:10,451
With a detector the size of a person, you
would wait about a hundred years to see one
neutrino hit.

17
00:01:13,027 --> 00:01:15,332
So the trick is not a cleverer trap.

18
00:01:15,332 --> 00:01:16,549
It is a bigger one.

19
00:01:16,549 --> 00:01:18,086
Think of shooting stars.

20
00:01:18,086 --> 00:01:21,736
Watch one small patch of sky, and you may
wait all night.

21
00:01:21,736 --> 00:01:24,681
Watch the whole sky, and you will see
several.

22
00:01:26,395 --> 00:01:29,364
Physicists wanted the rarest neutrinos of
all:

23
00:01:29,364 --> 00:01:32,010
the most energetic ones, from deep space.

24
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:36,011
Space also throws charged particles at us,
called cosmic rays.

25
00:01:36,011 --> 00:01:40,980
But magnetic fields bend their paths, so we
cannot tell where they came from.

26
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:45,497
Neutrinos are made in the same violent
places, and nothing bends them.

27
00:01:45,497 --> 00:01:47,433
They point straight back home.

28
00:01:50,461 --> 00:01:56,981
In the 1980s, Francis Halzen, a physicist at
the University of Wisconsin, had a bold
idea.

29
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:02,704
Why build a giant tank of water, when
Antarctica already has one, frozen solid?

30
00:02:02,704 --> 00:02:04,732
Deep ice is completely dark.

31
00:02:04,732 --> 00:02:08,427
No life, very little radioactivity, no
earthquakes.

32
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,160
The first test detector, called AMANDA,
brought a surprise.

33
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,862
The upper ice was full of tiny bubbles that
scattered light.

34
00:02:17,862 --> 00:02:22,058
But below about fourteen hundred metres, it
was astonishingly clear.

35
00:02:22,058 --> 00:02:24,526
A flash could travel hundreds of metres.

36
00:02:27,131 --> 00:02:31,127
So how do you put sensors that deep? You do
not drill. You melt.

37
00:02:31,127 --> 00:02:39,432
A hot water drill, like a giant shower head,
melted holes sixty centimetres wide, down to
two thousand four hundred and fifty metres.

38
00:02:39,432 --> 00:02:41,305
Each hole took about two days.

39
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:45,151
Then a race against the freeze.

40
00:02:45,151 --> 00:02:48,664
Crews lowered a cable of sensors in about
eleven hours.

41
00:02:48,664 --> 00:02:52,560
Within weeks the water froze solid, locking
them in for good.

42
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:56,456
Over seven Antarctic summers, they did this
eighty-six times.

43
00:02:59,019 --> 00:03:04,428
Today, five thousand one hundred and sixty
sensors hang in a cubic kilometre of ice.

44
00:03:04,428 --> 00:03:07,391
Halzen calls each one a light bulb in
reverse.

45
00:03:07,391 --> 00:03:10,289
It catches light, and turns it into a
signal.

46
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:17,069
But if neutrinos pass through everything,
what does the ice see?

47
00:03:17,069 --> 00:03:20,798
Very rarely, a neutrino smashes into an
atom's nucleus.

48
00:03:20,798 --> 00:03:27,240
The crash creates a new charged particle,
often a muon, flying on in nearly the same
direction.

49
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:30,631
Now the beautiful part.

50
00:03:30,631 --> 00:03:36,391
Inside ice, light slows down, and this
particle moves faster than light can move in
ice.

51
00:03:36,391 --> 00:03:41,824
So it leaves a cone of faint blue light,
like the V shaped wake behind a speedboat.

52
00:03:41,824 --> 00:03:43,395
This is Cherenkov light.

53
00:03:46,017 --> 00:03:48,201
Which way did it come from? Timing.

54
00:03:48,201 --> 00:03:52,696
The cone sweeps through the ice, and sensors
light up one after another.

55
00:03:52,696 --> 00:03:55,567
Connect the dots in order, and you get a
line.

56
00:03:55,567 --> 00:03:59,063
Follow it backwards, and it points to a spot
in the sky.

57
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:01,644
One problem.

58
00:04:01,644 --> 00:04:06,999
Particles from our own atmosphere rain into
the ice, over a hundred million a day.

59
00:04:06,999 --> 00:04:10,069
So IceCube also looks down, through the
planet.

60
00:04:10,069 --> 00:04:17,122
Almost nothing but neutrinos can cross the
Earth, so a track coming up from below is
very likely a neutrino.

61
00:04:19,863 --> 00:04:26,712
In 2013, the team reported the first
evidence of neutrinos with far too much
energy to come from here.

62
00:04:26,712 --> 00:04:29,330
They came from beyond our solar system.

63
00:04:29,330 --> 00:04:31,277
Neutrino astronomy had begun.

64
00:04:33,727 --> 00:04:39,136
On 22 September 2017, one neutrino arrived
with about 290 TeV.

65
00:04:39,136 --> 00:04:45,242
IceCube sent an alert, and telescopes around
the world turned to look.

66
00:04:45,242 --> 00:04:48,731
In that direction, a galaxy was flaring:

67
00:04:48,731 --> 00:04:56,757
the blazar TXS 0506+056, about four billion
light years away, powered by a giant black
hole.

68
00:04:59,904 --> 00:05:01,836
In 2022, a second clue:

69
00:05:01,836 --> 00:05:08,893
about 79 neutrinos from NGC 1068, a galaxy
whose black hole hides behind thick dust.

70
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:12,589
Light struggles to escape. Neutrinos do not.

71
00:05:12,589 --> 00:05:16,958
Scientists call it strong evidence, not
final proof.

72
00:05:19,746 --> 00:05:29,792
And in 2023, with machine learning on ten
years of data, IceCube drew the first
picture of our own Milky Way, not in light,
but in neutrinos.

73
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:36,969
On 6 October 2026, Francis Halzen won the
Nobel Prize in Physics.

74
00:05:36,969 --> 00:05:46,070
He called it a relief to finally deliver the
recognition that the whole collaboration
deserves, about 450 scientists today.

75
00:05:48,669 --> 00:05:50,116
And the hunt goes on.

76
00:05:50,116 --> 00:05:54,939
An upgrade was installed in 2025 and 2026 to
sharpen IceCube's vision.

77
00:05:54,939 --> 00:06:03,827
A proposed IceCube-Gen2 would watch eight
times more ice, and listen with radio
antennas for the most energetic neutrinos of
all.

78
00:06:06,502 --> 00:06:08,779
So look at your fingernail once more.

79
00:06:08,779 --> 00:06:11,733
Billions of ghosts are still passing through
it.

80
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:14,626
For almost a century, we could barely see
them.

81
00:06:14,626 --> 00:06:19,980
Now, at the bottom of the world, in total
darkness, a block of ice is quietly
watching.

82
00:06:21,804 --> 00:06:24,237
This is Latoon. Voyaging the unseen.

83
00:06:24,237 --> 00:06:26,874
Subscribe, and tell us in the comments:

84
00:06:26,874 --> 00:06:29,714
what invisible thing should we catch next?

